<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>PyTorch CUDA &amp; ROCm Compatibility Matrix — Versions, Drivers &amp; pip install</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Which CUDA, ROCm, Python and NVIDIA driver versions work with each PyTorch release? Search every official PyTorch build (CUDA, ROCm, Intel XPU, CPU) and get the exact pip install command. Latest: PyTorch {{ latest.torch }}.">
  <meta name="keywords" content="PyTorch CUDA compatibility, PyTorch CUDA version, PyTorch ROCm version, PyTorch Python version, NVIDIA driver version, torch cuda matrix, pip install torch cuda, PyTorch compute capability, AMD gfx">
  <meta name="author" content="Daniel Hou">
  <meta name="robots" content="index, follow">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#ee4c2c">
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://{{ domain }}/">
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="PyTorch Compatibility Matrix">
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://{{ domain }}/">
  <meta property="og:title" content="PyTorch CUDA &amp; ROCm Compatibility Matrix">
  <meta property="og:description" content="Find which CUDA, ROCm, Python and driver versions work with every PyTorch release — plus the exact pip install command.">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="PyTorch CUDA &amp; ROCm Compatibility Matrix">
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="Find which CUDA, ROCm, Python and driver versions work with every PyTorch release — plus the exact pip install command.">
  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {{ structured_data | safe }}
  </script>
  <script>
    // Apply the saved theme before first paint to avoid a flash.
    try {
      const t = localStorage.getItem("theme");
      if (t === "light" || t === "dark") document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t;
    } catch (e) {}
  </script>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
  <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22 viewBox=%220 0 100 100%22><text y=%22.9em%22 font-size=%2290%22>🔥</text></svg>">
</head>
<body>
<header>
  <button id="theme-toggle" class="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Toggle light/dark theme"></button>
  <h1>🔥 PyTorch Compatibility Matrix</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">
    Every official PyTorch build — CUDA, ROCm, Intel XPU and CPU — with the Python versions,
    platforms, minimum drivers and the exact <code>pip install</code> command.
  </p>
  <p class="meta">
    Data scraped automatically from
    <a href="https://download.pytorch.org/whl/torch/">download.pytorch.org</a>
    · last updated <time datetime="{{ generated_utc }}">{{ generated_utc }}</time>
    · {{ n_combos }} build combinations
    · <a href="https://github.com/DanielHou315/torch-compat">source on GitHub</a>
  </p>
</header>

<main>
  <section class="filters" aria-label="Filters">
    <div class="filter">
      <label for="f-torch">PyTorch</label>
      <select id="f-torch"></select>
    </div>
    <div class="filter">
      <label for="f-accel">Accelerator</label>
      <select id="f-accel"></select>
    </div>
    <div class="filter">
      <label for="f-accel-ver">Accelerator version</label>
      <select id="f-accel-ver"></select>
    </div>
    <div class="filter">
      <label for="f-python">Python</label>
      <select id="f-python"></select>
    </div>
    <div class="filter">
      <label for="f-os">OS / Arch</label>
      <select id="f-os"></select>
    </div>
    <div class="flex-break"></div>
    <div class="filter">
      <label for="f-gpu">Your GPU (optional)</label>
      <select id="f-gpu"></select>
    </div>
    <button id="f-reset" type="button">Reset</button>
  </section>

  <p id="gpu-note" class="gpu-note" hidden></p>
  <p id="count" class="count" role="status"></p>

  <table id="results">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>PyTorch</th>
        <th>Accelerator</th>
        <th>Python</th>
        <th>OS / Arch</th>
        <th>Requirements</th>
        <th>Install</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody></tbody>
  </table>

  <nav id="pager" class="pager" aria-label="Result pages"></nav>

  <section class="notes" id="versions">
    <h2>PyTorch versions at a glance</h2>
    <p>CUDA, ROCm and Python versions with official wheels for each stable PyTorch release.</p>
    <details>
      <summary>Show all {{ summary | length }} releases</summary>
      <table class="summary">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>PyTorch</th><th>CUDA</th><th>ROCm</th><th>Intel XPU</th><th>Python</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        {%- for r in summary %}
          <tr>
            <td>{{ r.torch }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.cuda | join(", ") or "—" }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.rocm | join(", ") or "—" }}</td>
            <td>{{ "yes" if r.xpu else "—" }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.python | join(", ") }}</td>
          </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="notes" id="faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {%- for q in faq %}
    <h3>{{ q.q }}</h3>
    <p>{{ q.a }}</p>
    {%- endfor %}
  </section>

  <section class="notes">
    <h2>Notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Minimum driver</strong> for CUDA rows comes from the
        <a href="https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-toolkit-release-notes/index.html">NVIDIA CUDA release notes</a>.
        Thanks to minor-version compatibility, a driver new enough for CUDA 11.x/12.x/13.x runs every toolkit of the same major version.</li>
      <li><strong>ROCm GPU lists</strong> are the architectures officially supported per
        <a href="https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/install-on-linux/en/latest/reference/system-requirements.html">AMD's documentation</a>;
        more consumer GPUs often work unofficially (e.g. via <code>HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>GPU filter</strong> compares against toolkit-level support — official wheels are sometimes built for fewer
        architectures (<code>TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST</code>), so treat results as an upper bound and check with
        <code>torch.cuda.is_available()</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>macOS</strong> CPU wheels include MPS (Metal) acceleration on Apple Silicon for PyTorch ≥ 1.12.</li>
      <li><strong>NVIDIA Jetson</strong> (Xavier, Orin) is Linux aarch64 only and uses
        <a href="https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/frameworks/install-pytorch-jetson-platform/index.html">NVIDIA's JetPack-specific wheels</a> —
        the aarch64 CUDA wheels here target SBSA servers (e.g. GH200).</li>
      <li><strong>Intel XPU</strong> wheels need <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/tool/pytorch-prerequisites-for-intel-gpu/2-9.html">Intel GPU drivers</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</main>

<footer>
  <p>
    Built on the work of <a href="https://github.com/elenacliu/pytorch_cuda_driver_compatibilities">elenacliu/pytorch_cuda_driver_compatibilities</a> —
    thank you, <a href="https://github.com/elenacliu">@elenacliu</a>!
    Maintained by <a href="https://github.com/DanielHou315">Daniel Hou</a>. Updated daily by CI.
  </p>
</footer>

<script id="dataset" type="application/json">{{ dataset_json | safe }}</script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
